 1. Pointer type decides how memory is read

A pointer stores an address. Its 'type' decides how many bytes are accessed and how they are interpreted (signed or unsigned).


#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i = 0;
    printf("i=%d\n", i);                 // i=0

    char *p;
    unsigned char *q;

    p = (char *)&i;
    q = (unsigned char *)&i;

    *p = 255;                            // writes 0xFF into the lowest byte of i
    printf("*p=%d *q=%d\n", *p, *q);     // *p=-1  *q=255
    printf("i=%d\n", i);                 // i=255 (only the lowest byte was written)

    return 0;
}


Why the outputs differ:

- `0xFF` read as `signed char` is `-1`.
- `0xFF` read as `unsigned char` is `255`.
- `*p = 255` changes only **one byte** of `i`. On a little-endian machine that is the lowest byte, so `i` becomes `255`.

Memory layout of `i` after the write (address 1000 is the lowest byte):


address:   1003       1002       1001       1000
         [00000000] [00000000] [00000000] [11111111]
                                              ^
                                          p, q point here


> Note: `char *p = &i;` (without a cast) gives an incompatible-pointer-type warning. Cast explicitly: `(char *)&i`.
> Storing 255 into a plain `char` is implementation-defined when `char` is signed. Use `unsigned char` when you mean raw bytes.



 2. Valid and invalid pointer assignments

`*p` is the 'value' at the address. `p` is the 'address' itself.


int i;
int *p;

*p = &i;        // INVALID: *p holds an int value, not an address
p = &i;         // valid:   p holds an address
*p = 100;       // valid:   (after p = &i) writes 100 into i

int *p2 = &i;   // valid:   assigning during initialization
                // here the * is part of the declaration, not a dereference


Note the two different meanings of `*`:

| Where                        | Meaning                                      |

| `int *p = &i;` (declaration) | declares `p` as a pointer to int             |
| `*p = 100;` (statement)      | dereference: access the value at the address |

So `int *p = &i;` is **not** the same as `*p = &i;`.



 3. Wild pointer

A pointer that is declared but 'never initialized' holds a garbage address.


int i = 10;
int *p;         // wild pointer: points to an unknown address

*p = i;         // INVALID: writes through an uninitialized pointer
                // undefined behavior (may crash, may appear to work)

printf("*p=%d\n", *p);   // undefined behavior, even if it prints 10


Even if the program appears to print `10`, that is luck. Behavior is 'undefined'.

Ways to avoid wild pointers:


int *p = NULL;      // initialize to NULL if no target yet
int *q = &i;        // or point it at a valid object right away

if (p != NULL) {
    *p = 5;         // check before dereferencing
}




 4. const with pointers

There are two things that can be fixed: the 'value' being pointed at, and the 'address' stored in the pointer.


int i = 10, j = 20;


 4.1 Pointer to constant: `const int *p`

The value cannot be changed through `p`. The address can change.


const int *p = &i;    // read-only pointer

*p = 200;             // ERROR: cannot modify value through p
p = &j;               // valid: p can point somewhere else


Read it as: "p is a pointer to a constant int."
`int const *p` means exactly the same thing.

### 4.2 Constant pointer: `int *const p`

The address is fixed. The value at that address can be changed.


int *const p = &i;    // constant pointer

*p = 200;             // valid: value can be modified
p = &j;               // ERROR: cannot change the address


A constant pointer **must be initialized** at the point of declaration, otherwise it can never be assigned later.


int *const p;         // bad: uninitialized and can never be set


 4.3 Constant pointer to constant: `const int *const p`

Both the address and the value are fixed.


const int *const p = &i;   // read-only constant pointer

*p = 200;             // ERROR
p = &j;               // ERROR


### How to read these declarations

Read right to left. Whatever `const` sits next to is what is fixed.

| Declaration    | Read as                     | `*p = x`   | `p = &j` |
|
| `int *p`       | pointer to int              | OK         | OK |
| `const int *p` | pointer to const int        | ERROR      | OK |
| `int const *p` | pointer to const int (same) | ERROR      | OK |
| `int *const p` | const pointer to int        | OK         | ERROR |
| `const int *const p` | const pointer to const int | ERROR | ERROR |

Rule of thumb: `const` **left of** the `*` protects the value. `const` 'right of' the `*` protects the address.



 5. Increment and decrement of pointers

Adding 1 to a pointer moves it by `sizeof(pointed type)` bytes, not by 1 byte.


#include <stdio.h>

int main(void)
{
    int i = 260;                      // 260 = 0x0104, assume &i is 1000
    int  *p = &i;
    char *q = (char *)&i;

    printf("p=%p q=%p\n", (void *)p, (void *)q);   // 1000 1000
    printf("*p=%d *q=%d\n", *p, *q);               // 260  4

    p = p + 1;                        // int pointer moves by 4 bytes
    q = q + 1;                        // char pointer moves by 1 byte

    printf("p=%p q=%p\n", (void *)p, (void *)q);   // 1004 1001
    printf("*q=%d\n", *q);                         // 1
    // *p now reads memory outside i: garbage / undefined behavior

    return 0;
}


Memory layout of `i = 260` (little-endian):


address:   1003       1002       1001       1000
         [00000000] [00000000] [00000001] [00000100]
                                    ^          ^
                                 q + 1     p, q (start)


- `*q` at 1000 reads `0000 0100` = **4**
- After `q = q + 1`, `*q` at 1001 reads `0000 0001` = 1
- After `p = p + 1`, `p` is 1004, past the end of `i`. Reading `*p` there gives 'garbage', because that memory does not belong to `i`.

### Pointer arithmetic rules

| Pointer type | `p + 1` moves by | `p - 1` moves by |

| `char *` | 1 byte | 1 byte |
| `short *` | 2 bytes | 2 bytes |
| `int *` | 4 bytes | 4 bytes |
| `double *` | 8 bytes | 8 bytes |

Decrement works the same way in the opposite direction (toward lower addresses). Sizes shown are typical; use `sizeof` to be sure on your platform.

> Print addresses with `%p` (cast to `void *`), not `%u`, so it stays correct on 64-bit systems.



 6. Quick reference

- &i is the address of i. *p is the value at address p.
- `p = &i;` is valid. `*p = &i;` is invalid.
- Never dereference an uninitialized (wild) pointer. Initialize to `NULL` or a valid address.
- `const int *p`: value fixed, address changeable.
- `int *const p`: address fixed (must initialize), value changeable.
- `const int *const p`: both fixed.
- `p + n` moves by `n * sizeof(*p)` bytes.
- Pointer type controls how many bytes are read and whether they are signed or unsigned.
